cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this playbook or its scripts, please cite it as below."
title: "VMFS Recovery: recovering VMware VMs from a bare VMFS6 datastore disk via WSL2"
abstract: >-
  A field-tested playbook and three scripts for recovering VMware virtual
  machines from a VMFS6 datastore disk that has been removed from an ESXi host
  and attached to a Windows PC over USB. Windows cannot read VMFS, so
  vmfs-attach.ps1 offlines the disk, passes it into WSL2 with usbipd-win, and
  mounts it with vmfs6-fuse. vmfs-copy.sh then copies each VM folder out,
  routing large -flat.vmdk disk images through GNU ddrescue (bad-sector retries,
  resumable via mapfile) and small .vmx, .nvram, and descriptor files through
  rsync, with folder detection, interactive selection, a live progress bar, a
  free-space check, and per-folder verification. verify-staged.sh is a read-only
  pre-flight on the result: exact image size against each VM's own descriptor
  extent, bad areas reported by the ddrescue mapfile, and the protective MBR and
  EFI PART GPT header read back off the destination copy rather than the source,
  which is the difference between a copy that finished and an image that boots.
  Covers crash and power-loss recovery mid-copy, migrating to a smaller host,
  and registering the recovered VM on an ESXi 8 host.
